{"metadata":{"tag":[{"Plain":"讲稿"},3],"author":[{"Lazy":[{"Local":{"url":"SimplicialCat","text":"SimplicialCat"}}]},4],"slug":[{"Plain":"notes-topology-1"},0],"ext":[{"Plain":"md"},1],"title":[{"Plain":"拓扑学 2026 秋助教讲稿 (1)"},2]},"content":{"Lazy":[{"Plain":"\n<h2>Finite topological spaces</h2>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>. Classify topologies on $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$ up to homeomorphism.</p>\n<p><em>Solution</em>. "},{"Local":{"url":"有限拓扑空间","text":"Finite topological spaces"}},{"Plain":" are equivalent to finite <strong>preorders</strong>. A topology on a finite set $X$ corresponds to the preorder $\\leq$ defined as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$x\\leq y$ if and only if every neighborhood of $x$ contains $y$, or equivalently, $x$ lies in the closure of $\\{y\\}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conversely, a preorder $\\leq$ on $X$ defines a topology by specifying a basis</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{\\uparrow x\\mid x\\in X\\}$, where $\\uparrow x$ is the subset $\\{y\\in X\\mid y\\geq x\\}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remark. When $x\\leq y$ we sometimes say that $x$ is a <strong>specialization</strong> of $y$ and that $y$ is a <strong>generalization</strong> of $x$, two terms from algebraic geometry. In algebraic geometry, one often consider "},{"Local":{"url":"一般点","text":"generic points"}},{"Plain":" on varieties (e.g. an affine line) and talk about their specializations. The closure of a generic point contains all its specializations.</p>\n<p>Moreover, under the above correspondence, finite "},{"Local":{"url":"T0空间","text":"$T_0$ spaces"}},{"Plain":" are equivalent to finite "},{"Local":{"url":"偏序集","text":"partial orders"}},{"Plain":". Non-$T_0$ spaces (preorders) can be made into $T_0$ spaces (partial orders) by identifying indistinguishable pairs, reducing the classification problem.</p>\n<p>To classify topologies on $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$, we first classify <em>connected</em> $T_0$ space, which correspond to connected partial orders.</p>\n<p>We list all connected partial orders on finite sets of cardinality at most $4$ (which are relatively easy to enumerate):</p>\n<p>Cardinality $1$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $2$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $3$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b &lt; c\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b, a &lt; c\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; c, b &lt; c\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $4$ (count: $10$)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b &lt; c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b &lt; c, b &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b, a &lt; c, a &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b, a &lt; c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b &lt; d, c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; c, b &lt; c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; c, b &lt; c, b &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; d, b &lt; d , c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b, a &lt; c, b &lt; d, c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; c, a &lt; d, b &lt; c, b &lt; d\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on this result, we may add indistinguishable points to existing ones to obtain a classification of connected <em>preorders</em> on finite sets of cardinality at most $4$ (write $a \\sim b$ for $a\\leq b \\land b\\leq a$):</p>\n<p>Cardinality $0$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\varnothing$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $1$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $2$</p>\n<ul>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $3$ (count: $6$)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>($3$ listed above)</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b \\sim c\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b &lt; c\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b \\sim c\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardinality $4$ (count: $21$)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>($10$ listed above)</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b &lt; c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b, a &lt; c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; c, b &lt; c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b \\sim c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b &lt; c, a &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b &lt; d, c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b &lt; c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a &lt; b \\sim c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b &lt; c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b \\sim c &lt; d\\}$</li>\n<li>$\\{a \\sim b \\sim c \\sim d\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A general topological space is the union of its connected components. Therefore the number of topologies on $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$ is</p>\n<p>$$\r\n\\underbrace{21}_{4} +\r\n\\underbrace{6}_{3+1} +\r\n\\underbrace{3}_{2+2} +\r\n\\underbrace{2}_{2+1+1} +\r\n\\underbrace{1}_{1+1+1+1} = 33.\r\n$$</p>\n"}]}}